# macOS
.DS_Store

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Generated reports and exports - rebuild them from the logs instead
*.html
ea_trades.csv

# Backtest results ARE kept - they are the record of what was tested and when,
# and a set file means little without the run that justified it. run_all.py
# collects only the small, diffable part: all_results.csv, comparison.csv and
# run.log. MT5's raw report_*.htm is tens of MB per grid, does not diff, and
# holds nothing the CSVs do not - so it stays out unless you pass
# --with-reports, and even then think before committing it.
!backtest-results/
backtest-results/**/report_*.htm
backtest-results/**/report_*.html
backtest-results/**/logs/
backtest-results/**/*.ini

# EA output. These are account data, not source: balances, equity and every
# trade. Never commit them, and never to a repo that might turn public.
SniperEA_Log_*.csv
SniperEA_Log_*.jsonl
*_state.txt

# MT5 input presets. These hold the live Telegram bot token and chat id, so they
# stay on the machine that runs the EA. A private repo is not a secret store -
# a token committed once lives in the history until the history is rewritten.
*.set
!*.example.set

# ...with one narrow exception: the BACKTEST GRID sets. These are parameter
# sweeps, not live presets - every one has InpUseTelegram=false and an EMPTY
# InpTgToken, and the runner globs the folder, so the grid is useless if they
# are not in the repo. The rule above still covers every set outside these two
# folders. Verify a set is token-free before adding anything else here.
!kz-grid/sets_kz/*.set
!kz-grid/*.set
!sweep-grid/sets_sweep/*.set
!sweep-grid/*.set
!turtle-grid/sets_turtle/*.set
!turtle-grid/*.set
